d has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319 :topic/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4ade3a-303a-5eec-9df3-e43d050e7127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5336b-989f-5a31-b653-6429b31a077f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4ade3a-303a-5eec-9df3-e43d050e7127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d64a07-1676-5fda-bef0-6a4849be81d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6722f30e-e2af-5a70-879f-cfff27425f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3d0a9a-fffb-56ce-a7bd-59c663e5f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6722f30e-e2af-5a70-879f-cfff27425f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb2af9-89d8-5f3d-8d83-90156885a877" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff5d22d-d9d1-5f3d-acef-b6be4a77c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} {:answer/id #uuid "bb044ed9-c0d7-5063-b76d-362678932554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff5d22d-d9d1-5f3d-acef-b6be4a77c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cba7c41-2ad1-5047-8e7d-0cc187a92400" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-177118" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319 :topic/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415149} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415157}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049606 :topic/id #uuid "03f0df59-343c-5dd7-ad4a-b7bacf1e1f44" :topic/math-academy-id 2105 :topic/title "Orthogonal Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018101f5-88ee-578c-a18f-ccb93c8d48a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/