er/id #uuid "367a2d75-1ecd-5ed6-b56c-88c44b0b8e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465843} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcad1d36-8c15-56ba-96ea-8a418c029efc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9622b4aa-6606-5dbb-91d5-63f920f6e03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21068" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n- **I.** $(16,8)$\n- **II.** $(8,2)$\n- **III.** $(-4,4)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478 :topic/id #uuid "a2bb3232-296a-5d16-b145-1ee30beae8b3" :topic/math-academy-id 1360 :topic/title "Using the First Derivative Test to Classify Local Extrema"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b1a512-445c-54f7-98eb-16e279f4f895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450176} {:answer/id #uuid "25b75876-10e0-5cad-a903-a2b32d7bf8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25b75876-10e0-5cad-a903-a2b32d7bf8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450177} :answer-field/id #uuid "866a13a4-47ef-589e-9287-4ecfb3752c32" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec6ba65a-d7ef-5a8b-9423-07c0be64f56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450178} {:answer/id #uuid "42a6b76c-7760-543f-a116-629e13103e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42a6b76c-7760-543f-a116-629e13103e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450179} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a9c080-f207-5bcb-a51b-c9d90694422f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a9b19a-2378-558a-aaba-669f8e9eab83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450180} {:answer/id #uuid "a83dc05d-fde3-56f2-a463-d124bd96c060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a9b19a-2378-558a-aaba-669f8e9eab83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450180} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eca5308-cf1b-50be-902b-cb69f775f6b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d001b69-269c-523e-bfae-f0f86907fed7" :question/math-academy-id "q-21467" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- **II.** $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- **III.** $x=1$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478 :topic/id #uuid "a2bb3232-296a-5d16-b145-1ee30beae8b3" :topic/math-academy-id 1360 :topic/title "Using the First Derivative Test to Classify Local Extrema"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7f0634-61fd-5c82-88da-7e629117634a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450182} {:answer/id #uuid "899d8517-fc94-54d3-9ecb-f90896d7de46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899d8517-fc94-54d3-9ecb-f90896d7de46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450183} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9d5e27-2edf-590b-89a2-5622f244e8c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c3829bd-23f7-5558-ad2d-ad25aa7aa625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450184} {:answer/id #uuid "f83818b6-de85-5ed1-a6ea-fd4bc327eefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83818b6-de85-5ed1-a6ea-fd4bc327eefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450185} :answer-field/id #uuid "7522a75c-221f-5f7e-ba8d-0b0b878f71a2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aac2952-926a-57ab-ba80-e57e635b4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450186} {:answer/id #uuid "43d49d7f-c711-5838-b3c3-f67c7b4e70b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aac2952-926a-57ab-ba80-e57e635b4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450186} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d5a316-4f26-5f0a-a784-27dddbbf543e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a70a3cbd-ee1f-54d1-83af-a484013d0e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-21468" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- **II.** $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- **III.** $x=0$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7695c96f-227a-5a24-96bb-98331d20ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450188} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b00ef5-2972-5565-9c8d-615fbdea7d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b00ef5-2972-5565-9c8d-615fbdea7d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450189} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec86aea-204a-537c-9265-e24eb853fbf5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "785b1360-8ef3-5e3d-bc20-b0fa4b4d9c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450190} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b54aef-16cd-5fa9-9ea9-5c40120eab94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b54aef-16cd-5fa9-9ea9-5c40120eab94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450191} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b01ba8-63a0-5562-83db-23726480b102" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285c8dc9-6285-500a-896b-acd2bd30c459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450192} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5e14c1-c34c-5064-bc85-8deb3ffd3502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285c8dc9-6285-500a-896b-acd2bd30c459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450192} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cbc9b4-055f-5e80-834a-95af75ee5fe0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a459992-6292-5256-9176-368a35c4e9be" :question/math-academy-id "q-21498" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+8x-5.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(-2)>0$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-2$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+8x-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x+8.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2beee5fa-9bef-5383-a9c4-8f197153726a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465845} {:answer/id #uuid "178776c8-5b13-58a3-af74-43b9a4920dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} 