d #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ddd5534-ec6d-5b82-a696-b9d68b20c5fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-22197" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0273afcf-1982-5569-8348-f10a8e77aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465881} {:answer/id #uuid "1526315c-74f6-5bb1-a15f-52ce2cf483f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1526315c-74f6-5bb1-a15f-52ce2cf483f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465882} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a11075d-7e91-53e3-8287-8ac5fd97acb2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c496d87e-e47e-5f9d-8298-d6c0c32cca85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465883} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f781a-5bc0-57ae-80e8-0675eb804587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f781a-5bc0-57ae-80e8-0675eb804587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88e12bf-9078-5424-8848-e2cb1e920461" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3910ecb-9299-5ff4-844f-6a07a3aa9e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465885} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbca59f-a430-5c3d-8b2e-b5eb1ff6889e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3910ecb-9299-5ff4-844f-6a07a3aa9e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465885} :answer-field/id #uuid "5770f9b6-676c-5c90-9086-6fea3b63ca67" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "527ec064-c6b0-5d4a-ac97-7dea39646f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-22495" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi , \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13944d0d-0205-593e-944f-2bafc4ef1f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450316} {:answer/id #uuid "1110776e-d3de-577c-b89d-218f7f3723bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1110776e-d3de-577c-b89d-218f7f3723bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450317} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0a42a3-232f-5936-a292-1979189a3dda" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de43c5ca-4492-5338-855f-154fefcf97d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450318} {:answer/id #uuid "d4161de8-f3f6-5f6a-ae8b-5edca7563c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4161de8-f3f6-5f6a-ae8b-5edca7563c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450319} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5e6af3-e2b5-573c-8cfd-64db38baa18e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ba6d0f-21ec-5098-b88d-4dccf8fd4026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450320} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0d28b2-4d0d-55ec-837e-7efca2ee7d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ba6d0f-21ec-5098-b88d-4dccf8fd4026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450320} :answer-field/id #uuid "126d5d96-834d-5b07-95d2-e535bd9d9ada" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b00d4d7b-8334-5175-b45a-76d83d5dab02" :question/math-academy-id "q-22561" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}).$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2-2{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2-2(0)^{2}}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162 :topic/id #uuid "840e1871-5648-5ed4-855a-86ff3a9aa7a4" :topic/math-academy-id 364 :topic/title "The Candidates Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efae998e-dc98-5b4b-ab4c-fc5995e8225f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450322} {:answer/id #uuid "f590a205-708b-5611-9105-ca206b5a0c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f590a205-708b-5611-9105-ca206b5a0c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450323} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dcbac18-fb46-5ad0-9978-9ee853a593d3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6755131-f931-5ec0-be6b-249ba3d2433f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450324} {:answer/id #uuid "f53f5ec6-26c9-594b-a1c5-a57d8d135618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6755131-f931-5ec0-be6b-249ba3d2433f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450324} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ebb718-81c2-5711-9fd5-d99025583547" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96fe261b-8924-5702-a08e-d644aaad551a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450326} {:answer/id #uuid "a6882046-d109-5fb1-9023-719192f9eec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96fe261b-8924-5702-a08e-d644aaad551a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450326} :answer-field/id #uuid "88b0c550-4739-517c-9c4a-5a5b493dbbd4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d406ba45-2233-5b01-8129-dbf69421606e" :question/math-academy-id "q-22568" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ defined on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}$\n- **II.** The global minimum value is $2\\sqrt{e}$\n- **III.** The global minimum value is $4\\sqrt[4]{e}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/98/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_kn