#edb/ref 17592186450332} {:answer/id #uuid "37bfcd8e-0e0b-51fc-9ec0-cf15499c42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37bfcd8e-0e0b-51fc-9ec0-cf15499c42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450333} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ffd325-1efe-5648-92b7-a6d4a306ac5f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba230de-9f3b-5a09-b2a3-45ae981f92cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-22580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})>0$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162 :topic/id #uuid "840e1871-5648-5ed4-855a-86ff3a9aa7a4" :topic/math-academy-id 364 :topic/title "The Candidates Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd2f387-78f6-5f81-a0b2-74b893aa84f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450334} {:answer/id #uuid "b462afe8-7a82-5de1-94cb-63d544016b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd2f387-78f6-5f81-a0b2-74b893aa84f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450334} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f48a609-bd28-5c48-bda2-7f7456aa7e99" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451121} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f59c79d5-8789-5c1b-938e-c7305ac7330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450336} {:answer/id #uuid "255a66d3-f0de-5672-ba3d-426e7335a82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "255a66d3-f0de-5672-ba3d-426e7335a82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450337} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4ecf85-8f6c-54ce-a92b-6f32afea6b97" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b78c07e-35a8-5942-953e-27aba4fb2e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450338} {:answer/id #uuid "82c0972b-3386-575d-bf4f-037b95f13919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c0972b-3386-575d-bf4f-037b95f13919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450339} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d9c0d2-e100-53e1-b0b2-381b1cf18674" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b73efa01-82ab-533b-98f8-7ad100b1bc59" :question/math-academy-id "q-22615" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- **II.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478 :topic/id #uuid "a2bb3232-296a-5d16-b145-1ee30beae8b3" :topic/math-academy-id 1360 :topic/title "Using the First Derivative Test to Classify Local Extrema"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cafc6fbb-0573-5869-9c44-be1ac95701f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450340} {:answer/id #uuid "1daa92e4-2740-545e-bd1a-c418fee4140d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1daa92e4-2740-545e-bd1a-c418fee4140d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450341} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4367404-af17-5477-ba53-0812e5c71498" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548db9d3-ad10-5553-b376-563ad2582f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450342} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7f7992-1940-53cd-a94a-701b90921502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548db9d3-ad10-5553-b376-563ad2582f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea3827c-dea0-5b29-bfe8-510e6e1724d9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e0b7cf-b5d2-5b98-9176-18a56dc27cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450344} {:answer/id #uuid "9e80045f-3eb2-5feb-a69b-4bbc47dca27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e80045f-3eb2-5feb-a69b-4bbc47dca27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450345} :answer-field/id #uuid "62918948-4ddf-580e-bfd0-86d21889aaeb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b51aa0-5a9a-5119-8333-c08e35e82d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-22754" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- **II.** $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- **III.** $x=2$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+2)-(x-1)\\cdot (2x-2)}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2-2{x}^{2}+4x-2}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ and $x=2$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{-(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}{((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-(1)^{2}+2(1)}{((1)^{2}-2(1)+2)^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{-(3)^{2}+2(3)}{((3)^{2}-2(3)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]
